init/main.c: start the AIC timer before the first kernel threads.

Until aic1_timer_init (an early_initcall) there is no clockevent. But
workqueue_init(), earlier in kernel_init_freeable(), makes kernel
threads, and kthread_bind() -> wait_task_inactive() sleeps one tick on
an hrtimer when the scheduler has preempted the new thread before it
slept. With no clockevent that sleep never ends: the J1 hung there,
blank screen, unless the early console was slow enough to change the
scheduling (earlycon=j1fb).

So kernel_init() starts the timer as soon as the AIC is re-armed and
interrupts are on; the early_initcall is left as a no-op fallback.

--- a/init/main.c
+++ b/init/main.c
@@ -1518,6 +1518,7 @@
 		void apple_aic1_quiesce(void);
 		void apple_aic1_hw_quiesce_quiet(void);
 		void apple_aic1_rearm(void);
+		int apple_aic1_timer_start(void);
 
 		p105_fb_dbg("init_go");
 		apple_aic1_quiesce();
@@ -1534,6 +1535,11 @@
 		p105_fb_dbg("thaw_on");
 		apple_aic1_release_escape();
 		p105_fb_dbg("esc_off");
+		/* The tick, before workqueue_init() makes the first kernel
+		 * threads: kthread_bind() can sleep one tick in
+		 * wait_task_inactive(), and with no clockevent yet that sleep
+		 * never ends (irq-apple-aic1.c, apple_aic1_timer_start). */
+		apple_aic1_timer_start();
 	}
 #endif
 #ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_APPLE_S5L
